acts, worker continuation and reconciliation complexity, but removes duplicated foreground/background supervision and makes cancellation, cleanup and evidence semantics consistent. M1 execution safety and M2 gate evidence ship together so the framework does not claim enforcement while raw CLI reviews can still satisfy formal gates. The two-phase proposal/import protocol adds one post-terminal mechanical step, but preserves the actual provider reviewer as evidence author and prevents controller self-absolution. Reusing shared Proof Admissibility adds registry work but avoids a second trust vocabulary and keeps gate-consumed facts centrally auditable. Legacy configured provider timeout ceilings remain separate and unchanged.\n\n## Conversational Texture\n\nThe user repeatedly emphasized that a ten-minute boundary must not cause an automatic restart, that silent but still-running agents should continue to be monitored, and that the target is host-independent cross-provider CLI execution rather than native subagent APIs. After iterative Claude Opus review and empirical Codex/POSIX probes, the user approved the consolidated design and explicitly asked to start a Story and implement it. The user values a framework-level solution at the provider CLI boundary: bounded observation, no duplicate restart, honest terminal/cancel truth, and proof that the exact provider review—not a controller-authored substitute—satisfied the formal gate.\n"
